# FixedNetSum — the statutory legacy as at the date of death (AEA 1925 s.46, Sch 1A)

The fixed net sum is the statutory legacy a surviving spouse or civil partner takes ahead of the
issue on intestacy (Administration of Estates Act 1925 s.46(1)(i), Table Case (2)(B)). The Act does
not state the figure: Schedule 1A delegates it to a Fixed Net Sum Order, and each Order replaces the
last from its own commencement date. So the answer depends on **when the intestate died**:

- **£270,000** for a death on or after 6 February 2020 — SI 2020/33, the Administration of Estates
  Act 1925 (Fixed Net Sum) Order 2020.
- **£322,000** for a death on or after 26 July 2023 — SI 2023/758, the Administration of Estates Act
  1925 (Fixed Net Sum) Order 2023.

The later Order **defeats** the earlier figure from its commencement date. That is written as a Catala
`exception`, not as an `if`, because it is genuine defeasibility: the base definition stays true for
every date the exception does not cover.

A death **before** 6 February 2020 returns `NeedsHuman`. Earlier figures are not modelled here, and
returning £270,000 for such a date would be a wrong answer presented as a computed one.

## Point-in-time citations

Every dated definition carries an `# eli:` line directly above it. The line is data, not prose:

- `value` must equal the money literal on the definition line below it;
- `effective` must equal the commencement date the definition is conditioned on, and every date
  literal in this file must be the `effective` date of some citation;
- `instrument` is the legislation.gov.uk identifier of the Order that sets the figure, as made;
- `pit` is the point-in-time ELI of the delegating provision (AEA 1925 s.46) on a date when the
  figure was in force, ending in that date.

`scripts/check-eli-point-in-time.py` enforces all four, and with `--resolve` it re-fetches each
identifier's machine-readable data from legislation.gov.uk: a withdrawn or moved version, a
commencement date that disagrees with `effective`, or an Order whose text no longer contains `value`
fails the build. The as-at figures themselves are asserted by the gov-oracle harness against
`tests/oracles/fixed-net-sum-aea1925.oracle.json`.

`IntestacyCascade` still takes the statutory legacy as a `context` default of £322,000. Feeding it
from this scope is separate work: clerk 1.2.0 does not resolve cross-module sub-scope calls.

```catala
declaration scope AsAt:
  input date_of_death content date
  internal fixed_net_sum content money
  output verdict content ComputedOrNeedsHuman.Verdict

scope AsAt:
  # eli: value=$270,000 effective=2020-02-06 instrument=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2020/33/made pit=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo5/15-16/23/section/46/2022-06-01
  definition fixed_net_sum equals $270,000

  # eli: value=$322,000 effective=2023-07-26 instrument=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/758/made pit=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo5/15-16/23/section/46/2024-06-01
  exception definition fixed_net_sum
    under condition date_of_death >= |2023-07-26|
    consequence equals $322,000

  definition verdict equals
    if date_of_death < |2020-02-06| then
      NeedsHuman
    else
      Computed content fixed_net_sum
```
